---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import LivingAvatar from '../components/LivingAvatar';
---

<Layout title="About · Tasin">
  <div class="pt-12 sm:pt-16 mb-24 grid md:grid-cols-[220px_1fr] gap-10 md:gap-14 items-start">
    <div class="w-40 md:w-full md:sticky md:top-24" aria-hidden="true">
      <LivingAvatar client:visible animate={false} />
    </div>

    <div class="min-w-0 animate-fade-in-up">
      <p class="t-label mb-4">About</p>
      <h1 class="t-title text-foreground mb-10">Most people call me Tasin.</h1>

      <div class="article">
        <p>
          I'm KM Khalid Saifullah. I build things I've watched people quietly route around.
        </p>

        <p>
          The longer version: I was answering tickets on the help desk at The College of Wooster, and learned that the licensed platform everyone was supposedly using had been slowly replaced by a patchwork of Slack threads and spreadsheets. So I interviewed 38 of the people living with it, built a replacement over a weekend, and pitched it to the four divisional directors. They said yes. That became AtlasDesk.
        </p>

        <p>
          Before the platform work, there was research — multi-agent coordination in autonomous vehicle routing, specifically the failure mode where vehicles talking to each other made traffic worse instead of better. I proposed a memory framework that resolved it and presented the work at NCUR. It's the kind of problem that taught me to trust instrumentation more than intuition.
        </p>

        <p>
          My interests sit at the seam between product thinking and systems design. I like problems that have been sitting in plain sight because the people living with them stopped noticing. I like shipping the thing, not writing about shipping the thing. I work fast with AI tools, and I'm stubborn about understanding what the code actually does before it reaches production.
        </p>

        <p>
          These days I spend less time adding features to AtlasDesk and more time running it. There are four engineers on it now, and most of what I do is review, decide, and make the boring parts mechanical — the lint rule that rejects a query pattern before it breaks the other database, the migration runner that counts rows before and after, the test that fails when a permission check goes missing. Shipping was the easy half.
        </p>

        <h2>Currently</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Leading the four college engineers who maintain AtlasDesk.</li>
          <li>Building an on-call agent for the platform, and the evaluation suite that decides whether it is allowed to ship.</li>
          <li>Writing up how my projects were built, from the product side and the engineering side.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
